<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gask Ridge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 1st century</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gask Ridge is a line of Roman forts, fortlets and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> watchtowers along a ridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, north of the Earn valley. Built probably in the 70s or 80s CE, during or soon after the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a>, it guarded the road towards the Tay and may be the earliest fortified land frontier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, older than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gask-ridge.jpg" alt="Gask Ridge" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: myself; derivative work by Singinglemon 18:44, 27 April 2011 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gask_Ridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name belongs to a ridge of land about 10 miles (16 km) long north of the River Earn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a>; in Scottish Gaelic a gasg is a projecting tail or strip of land. In the early 20th century a line of Roman signal or watch towers was found along it between the Roman forts of Strageath and Bertha. The wider system roughly follows the boundary between the fertile Lowlands and the mountainous Highlands, stretching from about Loch Lomond to Montrose, and lay well north of the later and much shorter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a>.</p><p>Its principal forts ran from Camelon in the south through Ardoch, Strageath and Bertha to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress of Inchtuthil and on to Cardean and Stracathro. A group in the south-west, Drumquhassle, Malling, Bochastle, Doune, Dalginross and Fendoch, became known as the glenblocker or Highland line forts, as they stand at the mouths of glens such as the Pass of Leny and the Sma' Glen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>All the forts were first built during the Flavian occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Tacitus wrote in his life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> that Agricola was fighting in the area around 80, and the latest coins date from 86, which suggests an occupation of six years at most. Yet excavation has shown that many forts were rebuilt, some twice, with no sign of destruction in war, an apparent contradiction that further digs may explain. The forts were long thought to bar invasion from the Highlands, but only Inchtuthil, with 5,000–6,000 soldiers, was strong enough to meet a major attack; the smaller posts more likely deterred raiding parties and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> rustling, while the road and its towers guarded the link to the harbours on the Firths of Tay and Forth.</p><p>Ardoch, Strageath, Bertha, Cargill and Dalginross have also produced Antonine material, showing reuse in the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a>, and the army passed through again in the Severan period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sixteen Roman watchtowers are known, among them Ardunie, Muir o'Fauld, Kirkhill and Gask House, several now only sites. Their design matches the towers on the River Rhine, and the Scottish ones are thought to have been built first. Modern excavation and interpretation have been led by the Roman Gask Project under Birgitta Hoffmann and David Woolliscroft.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gask Ridge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their territory lay close to the Roman frontier: forts along the <b>Gask Ridge</b> and the legionary base at Carpow on the Tay, built under Severus around 208, show heavy Roman activity.</blockquote><a href="/state/venicones">Venicones</a></li></ul></section>
